1. The law must protect the sexual and reproductive health of men no less than that of women, but because women bear the burden of gestation and their lives and health are at stake in pregnancy and childbirth than those of men, women are more centrally concerned than men in legal protection and promotion of reproductive health;

2. Women have the right to the necessary knowledge and means for the full exercise of their reproductive choice in accordance with the Constitution and their beliefs and preferences, (par. d, Art. 1 of the Women's Development Code of Davao City);

3. The human rights of women include their right to have control over and decide freely and responsibly on matters related to their sexuality, including sexual and reproductive health, free of coercion, discrimination and violence (par. 96, Platform for Action, 1995 Fourth World Conference on Women in Beijing, China);

4. The implementation of gender responsive development policies and an integrated and development support system are essential elements for the promotion of reproductive health rights;

5. Reproductive health rights seek to promote not only the rights and welfare of adult individuals and couples but those of adolescents' and children as well;

6. Efforts must be made for the active participation and effective partnership among the national government, local government units, non-government organizations, people's organizations and the private sector in the design, implementation, coordination, integration, monitoring and evaluation of reproductive health programs.

