(a) The City Government of Davao adheres to the principle that human rights are universal and inalienable. It also adheres to the principle of equality, equity, and social justice as it would give utmost respect to each and every individual, making sure that each and every person is protected by law;

(b) The City Government of Davao also advances the policy that Gender and Development is a collective commitment of both women and men against structures, systems and practices that perpetuate subordination, discrimination, marginalization, stereotypes and all forms of gender-based violence, fully aware and cognizant that men must take leadership roles as well as increasing the understanding of the gains of gender equality for themselves as individuals and as a group, as well as the benefits for society as a whole;

(c) Consistent with the 2009 UN Commission on the Status of Women Report, the City Government of Davao also recognizes "the capacity of men and boys in bringing about change in attitudes, relationships, and access to resources and decision-making which are critical for the promotion of gender equality and the fulfillment of women's rights." The City Government of Davao envisions this policy to "ensure that men and boys, whose role is critical in achieving gender equality, are actively involved in policies and programs that aim to involve the equal sharing of responsibilities."

(d) It is also the policy of the City Government of Davao as enshrined in the amended Women's Development Cicle, particularly Article XI, Section 5 which states: "Organizing of Men as Partners in GAD. All barangays in the city shall facilitate the organization of men within their locality to serve as partners of the BCW and the city, in general, in all of its GAD Advocacies and/or endeavors. Specifically, Men's Responsibility on Gender and Development shall include the following: to establish a barangay to collectively discuss, learn and initiate activities as men, to promote equality of men and women and to end all forms of discrimination and violence against women, hence, promoting peace and harmony in the community."

(e) The incidence of rape, incest, child abuse and violence against women have been steadily increasing over the years. In order to reverse this trend and establish a violence-free Davao City, the Government believes that involving men and boys as partners in gender and development programs, projects and activities, will be significant.

The City Government of Davao recognizes that while men are generally the users of gender-based violence, men are also important stakeholders if the end goal of eliminating domestic and other gender-based violence is to be achieved.

Men in the communities must be involved and equally be given an active responsibility in advocating for violence-free communities. Male peer organizers in every barangay must be convened, trained, and capacitated in educating other men in their communities on gender-based violence and how it can be eliminated.

Furthermore, male victims of gender-based violence such as, but not limited to, sexual harassment, acts of lasciviousness, incest, rape, and intimate relationship violence that are physical, psychological, sexual, or economic in nature may also file a complaint under this Ordinance or under any relevant national laws and local ordinances.

Gender desks personnel in all Philippine National Police (PNP) stations in the City of Davao must be equipped with skills in receiving, managing, and handling male victims of gender-based violence.

Gender-based violence is a form of discrimination. Acts of gender-based discrimination may include, but not limited to, the following:

